> ## Root cause
> 
> `@braintree/sanitize-url@7.1.1` assigns its named export *above* the function declaration, relying on JS function hoisting:
> 
> ```js
> // 7.1.1 dist/index.js
> "use strict";
> Object.defineProperty(exports, "__esModule", { value: true });
> exports.sanitizeUrl = sanitizeUrl;          // ← uses hoisted `sanitizeUrl`
> var constants_1 = require("./constants");
> function sanitizeUrl(url) { /* ... */ }
> ```
> 
> This is valid CJS in Node — the `function` declaration is hoisted, so `exports.sanitizeUrl` is the function by the time the module's CJS > exports object is read.
> 
> `7.1.2` reordered to assign *after* the function:
> 
> ```diff
> - exports.sanitizeUrl = sanitizeUrl;
> + exports.sanitizeUrl = void 0;
>   var constants_1 = require("./constants");
>   function sanitizeUrl(url) { /* ... */ }
> + exports.sanitizeUrl = sanitizeUrl;
> ```

This is the opposite. 7.1.2 has `exports.sanitizeUrl = sanitizeUrl;` before `function sanitizeUrl(url) {` and relies on the hoisting behavior.

